WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.020 --> 00:00:05.570
We are now ready to define the notion
of the Hidden Markov Model,

00:00:05.570 --> 00:00:13.420
and to do this, we will turn the dealer
into a machine with k hidden states.

00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:16.920
This machine will proceed
in a sequence of steps,

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:20.220
and, for example,
in the case of coin flipping, these

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:25.690
hidden states will correspond
to fair or biased coins.

00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:32.840
At each step, the machine emits one
of the symbols from the alphabet.

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:38.630
In the case of coin flipping, the symbols
the machine will emits will be heads or

00:00:38.630 --> 00:00:40.490
tails, of course.

00:00:40.490 --> 00:00:46.710
And, while in a certain state,
the machine makes two decisions:

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:49.330
"Which symbol will I emit?"

00:00:49.330 --> 00:00:52.250
(this is the first question
it has to answer),

00:00:52.250 --> 00:00:56.895
and "Which hidden state
will I move to next?"

00:00:56.895 --> 00:00:59.385
(this is the second question
it has to answer).

00:00:59.385 --> 00:01:05.845
And it uses probabilistic arguments
to make these two decisions.

00:01:07.105 --> 00:01:11.935
Now, why is the word "hidden"
in the "Hidden Markov Model"?

00:01:11.935 --> 00:01:16.490
An observer can see
the emitted symbol of an HMM

00:01:16.490 --> 00:01:24.690
but doesn't know which state the machine
was in when it emitted this hidden symbol,

00:01:24.690 --> 00:01:29.830
and the goal is to infer the most
likely sequence of hidden states

00:01:29.830 --> 00:01:33.990
of an HMM based on the sequence
of emitted symbols.

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:40.620
Formally, the Hidden Markov Model is
defined by the set of the following

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:42.340
four objects.

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:46.400
First, an alphabet of emitted symbols.

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:51.790
Second, a set of hidden states of the HMM.

00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:55.560
In the case of the crooked casino,

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:59.460
an alphabet of emitted symbols
will be heads or tails;

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:03.880
a set of hidden states will be
fair or biased coin.

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:07.980
Next, a matrix of
transition probabilities,

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:13.725
which describes the probability of
moving from state l to state k.

00:02:13.725 --> 00:02:18.100
In our example of the crooked casino,
this is the matrix.

00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:23.918
For example, the probability of moving
from state F to state F is 0.9,

00:02:23.918 --> 00:02:29.676
but, the probability of moving from
state F to state B will be 0.1

00:02:29.676 --> 00:02:33.957
because the probability of
switching coins is 0.1.

00:02:33.957 --> 00:02:39.320
And finally, the Hidden Markov
Model is described by matrix of

00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:45.000
emission probabilities that describes
the probability of emitting symbol b

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:48.500
when the HMM is in state k.

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:54.449
For example, for the crooked casino,
this matrix is shown on the slide.

00:02:54.449 --> 00:03:00.719
The probability of emitting heads
from the fair state is 0.5,

00:03:00.719 --> 00:03:07.847
but the probability of emitting
heads from the biased state is 0.75.

00:03:07.847 --> 00:03:12.350
We will represent HMMs by HMM diagrams.

00:03:12.350 --> 00:03:17.064
In the case of crooked casino,
our HMM has two states: F and B,

00:03:17.064 --> 00:03:21.350
and there are all possible
transitions between these states,

00:03:21.350 --> 00:03:25.859
and these transitions are described by
the transition probability matrix.

00:03:25.859 --> 00:03:29.450
This HMM emits two symbols: H and T,

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:34.240
and the probability of
emitting these symbols

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:38.005
is described by the emission
probability matrix.

00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:44.780
To study Hidden Markov Models, we will
define the notion of a hidden path.

00:03:44.780 --> 00:03:49.890
The hidden path is a sequence of
states that the HMM passes through,

00:03:49.890 --> 00:03:55.098
and we will denote it as:
pi = pi1 pi2 ... piN

00:03:55.098 --> 00:03:58.980
We will also define the
probability of (x,pi), aka Pr(x,pi)

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:02.324
which is the probability that an HMM

00:04:02.324 --> 00:04:07.015
follows the hidden path pi and
emits the string x.

00:04:07.015 --> 00:04:11.078
In this slide, you see
an example of string x

00:04:11.078 --> 00:04:12.657
and hidden path pi.

00:04:12.657 --> 00:04:17.973
Our goal is to compute Pr(x,pi).

00:04:17.973 --> 00:04:22.881
Note that if we sum up probabilities of

00:04:22.881 --> 00:04:26.959
(x,pi) through all possible strings x

00:04:26.959 --> 00:04:33.112
and through all possible paths P,
the result will be, of course, 1.

00:04:33.112 --> 00:04:36.502
So there are how many
sums times sums?

00:04:36.502 --> 00:04:41.359
It is 2^n choices of x

00:04:41.359 --> 00:04:44.606
multiplied by 2^n choices of pi.

00:04:44.606 --> 00:04:49.779
To compute Pr(x,pi),
we will need to introduce

00:04:49.779 --> 00:04:54.440
one more notion, which is the
probability of x given pi, aka Pr(x|pi).

00:04:55.460 --> 00:05:01.480
Pr(x|pi) is the conditional
probability that an HMM

00:05:01.480 --> 00:05:06.100
emits the string x after
following the hidden path pi.

00:05:07.150 --> 00:05:12.256
Please note that, in this
case, the sum through all possible

00:05:12.256 --> 00:05:19.260
emitted strings x of Pr(x|pi)
is equal to 1.

00:05:19.260 --> 00:05:22.940
Please notice the difference
between these two sums.

00:05:22.940 --> 00:05:26.710
The sum on the top is the sum of

00:05:26.710 --> 00:05:29.972
2^n choices of x multiplied by
2^n choices of pi,

00:05:29.972 --> 00:05:35.150
while the sum on the bottom is the sum

00:05:35.150 --> 00:05:40.350
of 2^n corresponding to
all possible emitted strings x.

00:05:42.290 --> 00:05:46.240
We are now ready to compute
the probability that an HMM

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:49.530
follows the hidden path pi and
emits the string x.

00:05:50.660 --> 00:05:57.840
To compute it, we need to define the
notion of probability of xi given pi_i,

00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:04.450
which is the probability that symbol
xi was emitted from the state pi_i.

00:06:04.450 --> 00:06:09.340
And this probability of course is equal to
the entry of the emission probability

00:06:09.340 --> 00:06:14.060
matrix that corresponds to emitting
symbol xi from the state pi_i.

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:20.090
Well, let's compute these probabilities.

00:06:20.090 --> 00:06:26.448
For the first column,
we have fair state emitting tails,

00:06:26.448 --> 00:06:30.950
which is, of course, 1/2,

00:06:30.950 --> 00:06:35.410
then fair state emitting heads,
which is, again, 1/2,

00:06:35.410 --> 00:06:37.950
fair state emitting tails, 1/2,

00:06:37.950 --> 00:06:43.610
and now biased state emitting heads,
which is 3/4.

00:06:43.610 --> 00:06:47.809
If we continue like this,
we will find out all these probabilities.

00:06:49.070 --> 00:06:55.460
Another thing that we need to compute to

00:06:57.700 --> 00:07:03.650
find Pr(x,pi) is the
probability of transitioning

00:07:03.650 --> 00:07:07.870
from the state pi_i to
the state pi_(i+1),

00:07:07.870 --> 00:07:12.334
Which is simply the transition
probability from the state pi_i to

00:07:12.334 --> 00:07:14.004
the state pi_(i+1).

00:07:14.004 --> 00:07:16.860
Let's start computing those probabilities.

00:07:16.860 --> 00:07:21.984
Well, at a first step, we transition
from fair state to fair state,

00:07:21.984 --> 00:07:24.174
with probability 0.9.

00:07:24.174 --> 00:07:28.934
At the next step, again from fair
state to fair state, again 0.9.

00:07:28.934 --> 00:07:34.318
But at the next step, we transition
from fair state to bias state,

00:07:34.318 --> 00:07:36.875
which has probability 0.1.

00:07:36.875 --> 00:07:38.523
We'll continue this way,

00:07:38.523 --> 00:07:42.940
and in this way, we will
compute all these values.

00:07:42.940 --> 00:07:44.900
We forgot about just one value,

00:07:44.900 --> 00:07:51.476
which is: What is
the probability that our HMM

00:07:51.476 --> 00:07:56.950
was in the fair or
biased state in the first moment?

00:07:56.950 --> 00:07:59.182
Now let's assume that
in the initial moment,

00:07:59.182 --> 00:08:05.320
HMM was equally likely to be either
in the fair state or the biased state.

00:08:05.320 --> 00:08:08.435
That's why we add this term 0.5.

00:08:09.620 --> 00:08:15.146
So after we filled this row,
we know that the probability of pi,

00:08:15.146 --> 00:08:19.730
the probability that HMM will
go through path pi,

00:08:19.730 --> 00:08:24.410
will simply be the product of
the corresponding transition probabilities.

00:08:24.410 --> 00:08:31.070
And what is, after we computed
these values, Pr(pi) and Pr(x|pi),

00:08:31.070 --> 00:08:36.200
we can compute Pr(x,pi),

00:08:36.200 --> 00:08:41.950
which is simply the product of all these
small probabilities that we computed;

00:08:41.950 --> 00:08:46.570
equal to Pr(x|pi) * Pr(pi).

00:08:48.360 --> 00:08:54.239
So the problem of computing Pr(x,pi)
has been reduced to two problems.

00:08:54.239 --> 00:08:58.534
The first problem is how to
compute Pr(pi),

00:08:58.534 --> 00:09:02.896
and the second problem is how
to compute the probability of an

00:09:02.896 --> 00:09:05.663
emitted string given a hidden path,

00:09:05.663 --> 00:09:11.653
and this is the problem of
computing Pr(x|pi).

00:09:11.653 --> 00:09:15.507
And we know now how to
solve both these problems.

